.TH CITERATORS 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
CITERATORS \- overview of the citerators API
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
citerators is one iteration API for cvec, chmap and cbmap. cvector.h,
chashmap.h and cbstmap.h each include it, so a translation unit that
includes one of those container headers has the full API and needs no
other #include.

ccol_for_each(3) is the usual form: it declares the iterator, walks the
container and destroys the iterator when the loop ends. You can also
write your own loop:
  - Declare the iterator with ccol_iter_declare(3).
  - Start it with ccol_begin(3).
  - Move it to the next element with ccol_iter_next(3).
ccol_iter_key_ptr(3) and ccol_iter_val_ptr(3) give typed pointers to
the current key and the current value.

ccol_begin(3) selects the begin function of the container from its type
at compile time, and a container of any other type is a compile error. A
container handle that is NULL iterates as an empty container.
.fi
.SH "KEYS AND VALUES"
.nf
Container        ccol_iter_key_ptr(3)        ccol_iter_val_ptr(3)
cvec of T        const size_t * (the index)  T *
chmap of K, V    const K *                   V *, or V const * when V
                                             is a character pointer
cbmap of K, V    const K *                   V *, or V const * when V
                                             is a character pointer
.fi
.SH "ORDER"
.nf
The iteration walks a cvec by index, from 0 up, and a cbmap in ascending
key order. A chmap has no defined order; chashmap_begin_iter(3)
describes it.
.fi
.SH "LIFETIME"
.nf
When ccol_iter_next(3) moves past the last element, it destroys the
iterator and gives NULL. A loop can stop before the last element, and
the iterator is then live until ccol_iter_destroy(3) or until the end of
the scope of ccol_iter_declare(3). The key pointer and the value pointer
are valid only while the iterator is on that element and the container
does not change. An insert into the container or a removal from it
during an iteration makes the iterator invalid, and the behavior is
then undefined.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macros use GNU C statement expressions and the cleanup attribute,
so they need GCC or Clang.
.fi
